#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134709} :activity/id #uuid "bff21f91-25dc-5812-8a0d-35d914692a82" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050684 :topic/id #uuid "5ec67949-5025-5014-b381-eb8a0f0199b6" :topic/math-academy-id 2171} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135064 :tutorial/content "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**." :tutorial/id #uuid "ac48d158-e2b0-57f9-b8ba-6fe587ca807a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3655 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "958cc7d8-671b-5ebd-9b9d-c938658e82bc" :step/math-academy-id 20314 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133617 :question/id #uuid "18e10da5-f3e1-52d2-b2c6-2a69a1bf1947" :question/math-academy-id "e-5614"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558577}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"} :step/id #uuid "9b6349ce-1de8-5cd8-aed5-641cee35b3ac" :step/math-academy-id 20315 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134891}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133618 :question/id #uuid "ab93579a-f7a1-5cfe-85ce-5a4d5722e7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5615"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eaee4e9-7be5-5c35-bc88-89697a15d75a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558552}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression That Passes Through the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "2aaa8ea5-3420-58b2-86f9-e413b60cb5d4" :step/math-academy-id 20317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135145 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "4e7ff642-aa4a-5099-ac22-42297dcfcacf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6153 :tutorial/title "Regression Planes Passing Through the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "4adafa4a-1969-530d-9be3-b8157c980149" :step/math-academy-id 37336 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134711}}] :activity/title "Multiple Linear Regression With Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134031}]}
